Wisdom doesn't have anything to do with your chances of successfully
reading a spellbook.  This is how it's done:

MAJOR spoiler...

It really is quite major...


OK, you asked for it.  If the spellbook's blessed, automatic success.
If it's cursed, automatic failure.  If it's uncursed, then the chance
of success is:

  (<your INT> + 4 + <half your level>) - (<twice the spell level>)

(That's half your level rounded down)
 
A random number is rolled on a d20, and if this is higher, then
you've got problems.  (It appears that rolling a 20 here fails no
matter what, so your chances of success are never better than 95%).
As an example, a 10th level wizard with 18 INT reading 'Finger of
Death' (7th level) would work out as:

  18 + 4 + 5 - 14 = 13, or a 65% chance of success.

While a 14th level barbarian with 8 INT reading the same book would
have:

  8 + 4 + 7 - 14 = 4, or a measly 20% chance!

